From 0386c81d8f620aa9e1c8a33156e18d91ea356f35 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:55:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [e-acsl] \object_pointer does not require the object to be
 writable

The ACSL definition of \object_pointer only asks the pointer to point
into an object, or one past its end: nothing requires the object to be
writable. The runtime implemented it with eacsl_valid, which is false
on a block marked read-only, so \object_pointer(p) was false for any
pointer into a string literal, a const global array or stdin, although
\valid_read(p) held. With -warn-invalid-pointer, the "invalid pointer"
guard (\null == p || \object_pointer(p)) then failed on the first read
through such a pointer.

Use eacsl_valid_read in eacsl_object_pointer, and \valid_read instead of
\valid in the behaviours of its contract, which stated the same
writability requirement (Eva concluded from it that \object_pointer is
false on a const array).

Regression test: tests/memory/object_pointer_readonly.c.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
